{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-ENGL30-Assist-Prof-English-TT-9-mos_2026-27-ad-final.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR, ENGLISH DEPARTMENT",
      "description": " \nASSISTANT PROFESSOR, ENGLISH DEPARTMENT \n(9-month, Tenure-Track Appointment) \nSeptember 2, 2026 \n \nSamford University’s Howard College of Arts and Sciences invites individuals interested in serving in a Christian \nuniversity environment to submit applications for a position as Assistant Professor of English, beginning \nAugust 2027. We seek a writer of fiction and/or nonfiction with secondary expertise in digital literacies, \ntechnologies of writing and research, and/or multimodal composition. This tenure-track position carries a \ntwenty-four (24) credit hour teaching load per academic year, as well as expectations for active research and \nservice. Teaching will include advanced and introductory courses for the Department and first-year writing \ncourses for the General Education program. A robust and realistic scholarly agenda is expected, as is service to \ndepartment, college, university, and community initiatives. \n \nQualified candidates must hold an MFA in creative writing and/or a PhD or ABD, near completion, in \nEnglish; have a publication record as a writer of fiction or nonfiction; and be prepared to equip students to \nnavigate the current digital landscape and utilize emerging technologies and AI. Applicants with \nsecondary expertise in digital studies, digital literacies, and/or digital media are encouraged to \napply. Applicants with training and experience teaching first-year writing are also encouraged to apply. \nSuccessful candidates will be committed to excellent teaching, student development and mentorship, and \ncollegial collaboration. ABD candidates will be hired at the rank of Instructor, with a commensurate salary, \nuntil Ph.D. is completed. \n \nEnglish studies has been a central focus of Samford University since the university’s founding in 1841, when \nthe original institution was called the Howard College of English and Classical Studies. Fourteen faculty \nmembers serve approximately sixty majors in a traditional BA program of writing and literary study, in optional \nconcentrations in Writing and Film, and minors in English, Film and Video Studies, and Writing. The \ndepartment supports a master’s degree program in Samford’s Orlean Beeson School of Education and \ncooperates with Samford University’s 3-3 program in the Cumberland School of Law. For more information, \nsee Samford’s English Department here.  \n \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally recognized \ngraduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 states, Puerto \nRico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation \nfor career preparation and #8 in the nation for student learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street \nJournal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian \nsetting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive \nsalaries with a generous benefits package. \n \nReview of applications will begin November 15, 2026, and applications will be accepted until January 15, 2027. \nPlease submit the following documents: 1) an application letter that includes statement of teaching \nphilosophy and experience and a description of research interests and activities, 2) a curriculum vitae with \ncontact information for three references, 3) an application for faculty position, and 4) the faculty applicant \nChristian mission statement. (The application and the statement are available at the links below.) Salary is \ndependent upon qualifications and experience. The successful candidate must pass a background check. \nApplicants must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visa \nfor this position. \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n \n\n“Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx  \n \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to:  \nFACAPP@Samford.edu  \nSubject: Asst. Prof., English  \n \nQuestions may be addressed to the search committee chair, Dr. Brad Busbee (mbusbee@samford.edu). \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, \nTitle IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans \nwith Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, \ngender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other \nprotected status under federal, state or local law applicable to the University, in its education policies, programs, \nand activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of the \nUniversity. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations concerning \ndiscrimination. \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-JMCO31-Revised-CAM-JMC-job-ad-TT-9-mos-Final.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Public Relations",
      "description": " \nAssistant Professor of Public Relations \nDepartment of Communication and Media  \n(9-month appointment, Tenure Track) \nSeptember 2, 2026 \n \nSamford University’s Howard College of Arts and Sciences invites individuals interested in serving in a \nChristian University environment to apply for an Assistant Professor of Public Relations position in the \nDepartment of Communication and Media. This full-time tenure track position is a 9-month appointment \nand carries a twenty-four (24) credit-hour teaching load. The position will begin in August 2027. \nQualified candidates will hold a Ph.D. or be ABD, near completion, in mass communication, \ncommunication, or a closely related field from an institutionally accredited college or university (or \nequivalent). Candidates with relevant professional experience and ability to teach a variety of public \nrelations courses including principles of public relations, public relations writing, and public relations \ncampaigns are preferred. Additional expertise in brand and content strategy, and/or strategic \ncommunication, advertising and content creation a plus. Successful candidates will have demonstrated a \nrecord of peer-reviewed scholarship and excellence in teaching. ABD candidates will be considered at the \nrank of Instructor, with commensurate salary, until Ph.D. is completed. \nThe Department of Communication and Media is home to Samford’s award-winning student media and \nhosts an annual speaker forum (Robinson Forum - a lecture series in cooperation with The Washington \nPost). Our Public Relations program is CEPR certified. \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading \nChristian university offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of \nnationally recognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 \nstudents from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time \nfaculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #8 in the nation for student learning \nopportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of \nrigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of \nthe Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \nReview of applications will begin October 9, 2026, and applications will continue to be accepted \nuntil October 23, 2026. Please submit (via email below in .pdf format), an application letter, curriculum \nvitae with names and contact information of three references, description of teaching experience and \ninterests, description of research plans, along with an Application for Faculty Position and the Faculty \nApplicant Christian Mission Statement (links below). Salary is dependent upon qualifications and \nexperience. The successful candidate must pass a background check. Applicants must be authorized to \nwork in the United States, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position. \nApplication for Faculty Position can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n \nFaculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n \n \n \n\nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to:  \nFACAPP@samford.edu  \nSubject: Public Relations Faculty \n \nQuestions may be addressed to:  \nDr. Ignatius Fosu, Search Committee Chair \n ifosu@samford.edu  \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of \n1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the \nAmericans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate \non the basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran \nstatus, religion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law applicable to the \nUniversity, in its education policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment \npolicies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As a faith-based institution, the University is \nexempted from certain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-Biology-adjunct-NTT-Part-time-advert-2026-002.pdf": {
      "title": "Part-Time Biology Adjunct Instructor",
      "description": " \nPart-Time Biology Adjunct Instructor \n \nSamford University’s Howard College of Arts and Sciences invites individuals interested in serving in a Christian University \nenvironment to apply for the position of part-time instructor in The Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences. \nThis is a part-time, non- tenure-track appointment and will begin in August 2026. \nThis position is expected to teach a variety of introductory biology courses. Responsibilities include delivering both lectures \nand laboratory sessions for classes such as Introductory Biology for non-majors, Microbiology, and Anatomy and \nPhysiology. The role involves creating and presenting coursework in ways that are engaging, innovative, and aligned with the \ndiscipline, while also reflecting the college’s core values, demonstrating an understanding of culturally responsive teaching, \nand showcasing expertise in the subject area. The candidate must adhere to professional faculty standards as outlined by \ncollege policies and procedures. A master’s degree in biology or related field is required, although a doctoral degree is \npreferred. Also, individuals with prior university teaching experience will be given preference. \nReview of applications will begin immediately and positions will remain open until filled. Applicants should submit a letter \nof application stating the qualifications for the position. In addition, please submit a brief statement of teaching philosophy, \na current CV/resume, application for faculty position (link below), faculty applicant Christian mission statement (link below), \nand a list of three professional references with contact information. The successful candidate must pass a background check. \nApplicants must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this \nposition. \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n“Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-Statement.docx \nPlease submit all application materials in PDF format to: \naoverton@samford.edu \nSubject: Biology Adjunct \n \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian university offering \nundergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally recognized graduate and professional \nschools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic \nschools with 450 full-time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #7 in the nation for \nstudent learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of \nrigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith \nand offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of the Education \nAmendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the \nUniversity does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, \nveteran status, religion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law applicable  to the University, in its education \npolicies, programs, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As a \nfaith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n"
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-Core-Writing-Part-Time-Instructor-Positions-F26-Final.pdf": {
      "title": "(Part-time Faculty – Writing, non-tenure track)",
      "description": " \n  \n  \n \n                    (Part-time Faculty – Writing, non-tenure track)                \n                      April 25, 2026 \nSamford University’s Howard College of Arts and Sciences invites individuals interested in serving in a Christian \nUniversity environment to apply for the position of part-time instructor in the Core Writing program. This is a \npart-time, non- tenure-track appointment and will begin in August 2026. \nQualified candidates should hold a Master’s degree in English or a closely related field with some     experience \nteaching first -year writing at  the college level. Responsibilities will include teaching first-year writing \ncourses in the program’s two-course sequence.  \n \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian university \noffering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally recognized graduate and \nprofessional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries \nin its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and \n#7 in the nation for student  learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is \ndedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers \nemployee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \n \nPlease submit a letter of application, a current CV , application for faculty position (link below), faculty applicant \nChristian mission statement (link below), and a list of three professional references with contact information. \nApplicants must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for \nthis position.  Prior to hire applicants must have a successful background check. \nPlease submit all application materials in PDF format to:  \nDr. Jennifer Ellis West, Director of Core Writing, at jennifer.west@samford.edu \nSubject:  Part-time Core Writing Instructor \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n“Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” \n      https://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-Statement.docx \n \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of \nthe Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans with Disabilities Act \nand ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, gender, race, color, national \norigin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other protected status under federal, state or \nlocal law applicable to th e University, in its education policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions policies, in \nemployment policies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As a faith- based institution, the University is \nexempted from certain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n \n"
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-Spanish-AdjunctforSpanish26.pdf": {
      "title": "Position: Part-Time, Adjunct Instructor, Non-Tenure Track",
      "description": " \nDiscipline: Spanish \nPosition: Part-Time, Adjunct Instructor, Non-Tenure Track \nStart Date: Fall 2026 \nPosting Date: November 15, 2025 \nSamford University’s Howard College invites applicants interested in serving in a Christian \nuniversity environment to apply for the position of Adjunct Instructor of Spanish. This part time \nposition will begin in January 2026. \nThe candidate should have an MA in the area of Spanish Language or a related ﬁeld from an \ninstitutionally accredited university (or equivalent). Highly qualiﬁed candidates with exceptional \nprofessional experience within the discipline may also be considered without having an MA. The \ncandidate will be expected to teach 100 and 200 level of Spanish courses as part of the Bachelor of \nArt (BA) in, and other Howard College core courses, which may include cross disciplinary \ncollaboration.  \nReview of applications will begin immediately, and position will remain open until ﬁlled. \nApplicants should submit a letter of application stating the qualiﬁcations for the position. In \naddition, please submit a brief statement of teaching philosophy, a current CV/resume, application \nfor faculty position (link below), faculty applicant Christian mission statement (link below), and a \nlist of three professional references with contact information.  \nThe successful candidate must pass a background check. Applicants must be authorized to work in \nthe United States, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position.  \nLinks to: \n“Application for Faculty Position” \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/ﬁles/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n and the \nFaculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement  \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n \nPlease submit all materials via e-mail in PDF or Word format to: \nkcjensen@samford.edu, Subject: Adjunct Instructor of Spanish \n\nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading \nChristian university offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of \nnationally recognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 \nstudents from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time \nfaculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #7 in the nation for \nstudent learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is \ndedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks \nand prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith. \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act \nof 1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, \nand the Americans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully \ndiscriminate on the basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic \ninformation, veteran status, religion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law \napplicable to the University, in its education policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions \npolicies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As a faith-\nbased institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations concerning \ndiscrimination "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/HCAS-Adjunct-for-WLAC-175.pdf": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor – Exploring Global Cultures (WLAC 175)",
      "description": " \n \n \n \nAdjunct Instructor – Exploring Global Cultures (WLAC 175) \nPart-Time, Non-Tenure Track \nStart Date: August 2026 \nDepartment: World Languages & Cultures \nPosted:  April 2, 2026 \n \nSamford University’s Howard College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for Adjunct Instructors to teach \nWLAC 175 – Exploring Global Cultures: Perspectives, Skills, and Engagement. This interdisciplinary, \n100-level course introduces students to intercultural competence, global perspectives, and entry-level \nlanguage or communicative practices in cultural and professional contexts. The course emphasizes cultural \nanalysis, reﬂection, and experiential learning rather than advanced language mastery. Language proﬁciency \nsupports cultural learning outcomes but is not the primary focus of instruction. \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford ( samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally recognized \ngraduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 states, Puerto Rico \nand 16 coun tries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full -time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for \ncareer preparation and #7 in the nation for student learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal  \n(2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The \nUniversity seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a \ngenerous beneﬁts package. \nQualiﬁed candidates should hold a master’s degree in World Languages & Cultures, Applied Linguistics, \nForeign Language Education, or a related ﬁeld paired with documented graduate coursework and/or \nprofessional experience relevant to intercultural competence, global engagement, or applied cultural \ncommunication. The professional experience could be experience living, working, teaching, or training in \ncross-cultural or international settings among other things.  \n \nPreference will be given to candidates who have experience teaching the language they propose to \nincorporate into the course or native speakers of the proposed language who have experience teaching any \nlanguage.  \n \nReview of applications will begin immediately, and position will remain open to establish a pool of qualiﬁed \ncandidates to ﬁll this need.  \n \nThe successful candidate must pass a background check. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United \nStates, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position.   \n \nApplicants should submit  the following: \n• A letter of application stating the qualiﬁcations for the position.  \n• A brief statement of teaching philosophy \n• A current CV/resume \n• An application for faculty position (link below) \n• A faculty applicant Christian mission statement (link below) \n• A list of three professional references with contact information.   \n \nPlease submit all materials via e-mail in PDF or Word format to:  \nDr. Kelly Jensen kcjensen@samford.edu  \nSubject: Adjunct Instructor of WLAC 175 \n\n \n \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/ﬁles/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n \n“Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-Statement.docx \n \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, \nTitle IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans \nwith Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, \ngender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other \nprotected status under federal, state or local law applicable to the University, in its education policies, \nprograms, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of \nthe University. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations \nconcerning discrimination. "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/BSOB-Accounting-Dept-Chair-Final-Aug-2027-Start.pdf": {
      "title": "Department Chair and Professor/Associate Professor of Accounting",
      "description": " \nDepartment Chair and Professor/Associate Professor of Accounting   \n(9-month, Tenure Track) \nApril 23, 2026 \nRevised:  To extend application deadline from September 6, 2026 to October 31, 2026. \n \nSamford University’s Brock School of Business invites applications from individuals interested in serving \nin a Christian university environment for a Department Chair position in the Department of Accounting, \nat the rank of Professor or Associate Professor . This tenure -track, nine-month faculty appointment will \nbegin in August 2027. \nApplicants must demonstrate strong teaching effectiveness and a sustained commitment to scholarly \nresearch. The standard teaching load for the Department Chair is two courses per semester. Research \nexpectations are aligned with the teaching load and are considered reasonable. Opportunities for summer \nteaching and competitive research grants are available.  Candidates should exhibit a strong commitment \nto academic excellence and the ability to work collaboratively and collegially with faculty, students, and \nexternal stakeholders. A terminal degree (Ph.D. or DBA) in Accounting from an AACSB -accredited \ninstitution is required, along with demonstrated academic leadership experience.  The Department Chair \nis responsible for leading the academic, administrative, and strategic initiatives of the department. This \nincludes oversight of undergraduate and graduate curriculum development, faculty recruitment and \nevaluation, student recruitment , external engagement, and budget management. The Chair also serves \nas the primary liaison between department faculty and the Dean. A CPA designation is preferred.  \nAssociate Professor candidates should have seven or more years of experience as a full -time faculty \nmember, an established record of published research and an active research agenda that would allow the \ncandidate to be classified as Scholarly Academic base d on the Brock School’s AACSB classification rubric. \nProfessor candidates should have all of the qualifications for Associate as noted above in addition to a \ndemonstrated excellence in teaching, research and service and a minimum of five years in the rank of \nAssociate Professor. \nThe Accounting Department enjoys a strong regional and national reputation for the quality of its \nprograms, as well as the success of its students on the CPA exam and in securing positions with Big Four \nand leading regional and local firms. In 2023, the program achieved a 78.3% first-time CPA exam pass rate, \nplacing Samford University among the top 5% of accounting programs nationwide. The department offers \nboth undergraduate and graduate degrees in accounting.  \nBrock School of Business is located in 75,000 -square-foot Cooney Hall, a state -of-the-art building that \nprovides excellent space for faculty to interact with students, colleagues and the regional business \ncommunity. Brock School of Business is accredited b y AACSB International, and hosts more than 1,200 \nstudents in its undergraduate, M.Acc. and M.B.A. programs. For more information, please go to \nsamford.edu/business.  \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally \nrecognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 \nstates, Puerto Rico and 16 coun tries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full -time faculty. Samford is \n\nranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #7 in the nation for student  learning opportunities as \nlisted in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic \ninquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith \nand offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \nSearch committee members will be attending the AAA Meetings in Las Vegas and encourage prospective \napplicants to connect through the AAA Career Center to arrange in -person meetings during the \nconference. Applications will be accepted immediately and continue through October 31, 2026. Review \nof applications will begin in September, but all applications received before the deadline will be \nconsidered. Application materials should include a cover letter detailing teaching and professional \ninterests and areas of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a completed faculty application, a faculty applicant \nChristian Mission Statement , and a list of t hree references  with contact information . Salary is \ncommensurate with qualifications and experience. The successful candidate must pass a background \ncheck and must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford University will not provide visa \nsponsorship for this position.  Samford University and the Brock School of Business are committed to \nfostering a vibrant learning environment among faculty, students, and staff, and strongly encourage \napplications from individuals who will contribute to the richness of the academic community. \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n \nand the “Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n  \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to: \nFACAPP@samford.edu \nSubject:  Accounting Chair  \nFor questions about the position please contact: \nDr. William H. Belski, Associate Professor of Accounting and Search Committee Co-Chair \nwhbelski@samford.edu \nBrandi Holley, Associate Professor of Accounting and Search Committee Co-Chair \nbholley2@samford.edu \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of \n1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the \nAmericans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendme nts, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on \nthe basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, \nreligion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law applicable to th e University, in its \neducation policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, \nand all other areas of the University. As a faith -based institution, the University is exempted from certain \nlaws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/DIV-New-Testament-Greek-Asst-Prof-9-mos-TT-Final.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Divinity (New Testament/Greek)",
      "description": " \n \n \nAssistant Professor of Divinity (New Testament/Greek) \n(9-Month, Tenure Track) \nSeptember 28, 2026 \n \nBeeson Divinity School of Samford University seeks an emerging scholar who demonstrates promise of \nexcellence in teaching, scholarship, and student interaction to serve as Assistant Professor of Divinity \n(New Testament/Greek) on a tenure-track, nine-month appointment, to begin in August 2027. Salary is \ncommensurate with qualifications and experience.  \n \nThis faculty member will teach a full load of graduate-level courses annually (typically 12 credit hours), \nas assigned by the Dean or Associate Dean. He or she will participate fully in the community life \nactivities of Beeson Divinity School, including but not limited to weekly services of corporate worship \nand faculty-led mentor groups. He or she will engage productively in research and scholarship to \ncontribute to scholarly discussions in the discipline. He or she will fulfill the normal functions of a full-\ntime divinity faculty member within the period of the nine-month appointment, including but not limited \nto keeping posted office hours, attending divinity and university faculty meetings, convocations, \ncommencement events, lectureships, etc., evaluating student’s academic performance and submitting \ngrades according to university policy, and serving on divinity and university committees as requested or \nappointed. He or she will serve collaboratively with other members of the Beeson faculty and staff to \nadvance the mission of the School and Samford University. \n \nConsideration will be given to applicants who are devoted followers of Jesus Christ, active in worship and \nservice in a local church, who agree with Beeson Divinity School’s statement of faith (see Samford \nFaculty Handbook, Section A5.1) and support the school’s evangelical and interdenominational ethos, and \nwho hold a research doctoral degree in New Testament Studies from an accredited institution. He or she \nmust be able to offer pastoral support and spiritual encouragement to students as they prepare for \nvocational ministry. A commitment to academic excellence and the ability to work effectively with \nfaculty, students and colleagues is expected. \n \nBeeson Divinity School (beesondivinity.com) was established in 1988 through the generosity of Ralph \nWaldo Beeson. The mission of the school is to prepare God-called persons to serve as ministers in the \nchurch of Jesus Christ by providing quality theological education in a community that worships the triune \nGod and cultivates authentic Christian spirituality. Beeson offers the Master of Divinity, the Master of \nArts in Theological Studies, the Master of Arts in Christian Counseling, the Master of Theology, and the \nDoctor of Philosophy. All degrees are accredited by the Commission on Accrediting of the Association of \nTheological Schools (COA/ATS). Beeson is one of ten schools that comprise Samford University. \n \nLocated in in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading \nChristian university offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of \nnationally recognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 \nstudents from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time \nfaculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #7 in the nation for \nstudent learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to \nthe promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers \nemployee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits \npackage. \n\n \nApplication deadline is November 30, 2026.  Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States, \nas Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position.  The successful candidate must pass \na background check. Application materials should be sent to FACAPP email as noted below. \n \nPlease submit the following as a single PDF:  1) cover letter, 2) application for faculty position (link \nbelow), 3) faculty applicant Christian mission statement (link below), and 4) curriculum vitae - (combined \ninto one PDF) to: \n \nFACAPP@samford.edu  \nSubject line: New Testament/Greek Search \n \nAdditionally, please have three letters of recommendation submitted directly from the \nrecommender to: jlinebau@samford.edu with the subject line denoting: “LAST NAME, FIRST \nNAME – New Testament/Greek Search.”    \n   \nThe “Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty- employment.pdf \n \nThe “Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant- Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n \nQuestions may be addressed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Jonathan Linebaugh \n(jlinebau@samford.edu). \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, \nTitle IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans \nwith Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, \ngender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other \nprotected status under federal, state or local law applicable to the University, in its education policies, \nprograms, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of \nthe University. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations \nconcerning discrimination. \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/DIV-Preaching-Prof-Assoc-Full-9-mos-TT-Final.pdf": {
      "title": "(Associate or Full Professor, 9-month, tenure track)",
      "description": " \n \n \nPreaching Professor and Director of the Robert Smith Jr. Preaching Institute \n(Associate or Full Professor, 9-month, tenure track) \nOctober 2, 2026 \n \nBeeson Divinity School of Samford University, Birmingham, Alabama, seeks a nationally/internationally \ninfluential homiletical leader and experienced practitioner in Preaching to fill a full-time faculty position, \nat the associate or full professor rank, on a 9-month, tenure track appointment, beginning August 2027. \nThis individual will also serve in an administrative capacity as director of the Robert Smith Jr. Preaching \nInstitute. \n \nThis faculty member will teach a full load of graduate-level courses annually (typically 12 credit hours), \nas assigned by the Dean or Associate Dean. He or she will participate fully in the community life \nactivities of Beeson Divinity School, attending weekly services of corporate worship, participating in \nweekly community lunches, and meeting with a small group of students weekly for mentoring purposes. \nHe or she will engage productively in research and scholarship to contribute to scholarly discussions in \nthe discipline. He or she will provide leadership in the school’s efforts to support and guide various \nstudent groups. He or she will fulfill the normal functions of a full-time divinity faculty member within \nthe period of the nine-month appointment, including but not limited to keeping posted office hours, \nattending divinity and university faculty meetings, convocations, commencement events, lectureships, \netc., evaluating student’s academic performance and submitting grades according to university policy, and \nserving on divinity and university committees as requested or appointed. He or she will serve \ncollaboratively with other members of the Beeson faculty and staff to advance the mission of the School \nand Samford University. \n \nAs director of the Robert Smith Jr. Preaching Institute (RSPI), this individual will lead in the planning, \nimplementation, and evaluation of RSPI programs and services. Established in 2018, the RSPI exists to \nserve and strengthen the whole person in relation to God, the church, other preachers, and the world for \nfaithful proclamation of God’s Word. The RSPI works to enrich the preparation of students for faithful \npreaching ministries, enhance the capacities and skills of current preachers, and exhibit and celebrate \nexemplary preaching and preachers. \n \nQualified candidates will have a doctorate in a relevant field, substantial preaching experience, and a \nproven record of effective classroom teaching. To be considered at the rank of full professor, the candidate \nmust demonstrate a strong record of high-quality publications and/or conference presentations appropriate \nto the field of homiletics. Qualified candidates must also evidence vibrant faith in Jesus Christ, orthodox \ndoctrinal convictions (demonstrated in an appropriate response to the 1963 Baptist Faith and Message), \nactive involvement in church life, a commitment to the evangelical intellectual and spiritual tradition, \nwillingness to mentor students and equip other Christian ministers for preaching and pastoral ministries, \nand a desire to serve with an interdenominational, evangelical faculty and staff.   \n \nBeeson Divinity School (beesondivinity.com) was established in 1988 through the generosity of Ralph \nWaldo Beeson. The mission of the school is to prepare God-called persons to serve as ministers in the \nchurch of Jesus Christ by providing quality theological education in a community that worships the triune \nGod and cultivates authentic Christian spirituality. Beeson offers the Master of Divinity, the Master of \nArts in Theological Studies, the Master of Arts in Christian Counseling, the Master of Theology, and the \n\nDoctor of Philosophy. All degrees are accredited by the Commission on Accrediting of the Association of \nTheological Schools (COA/ATS). Beeson is one of ten schools that comprise Samford University. \n \nLocated in in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading \nChristian university offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of \nnationally recognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 \nstudents from 44 states, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time \nfaculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation and #7 in the nation for \nstudent learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to \nthe promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers \nemployee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits \npackage. \n \nApplication deadline is November 30, 2026.  Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States, \nas Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position.  The successful candidate must pass \na background check. Application materials should be sent to FACAPP email as noted below. \n \nPlease submit 1) cover letter, 2) application for faculty position (link below), 3) faculty applicant \nChristian mission statement (link below), and 4) curriculum vita - (combined into one PDF) to: \nFACAPP@samford.edu  \nSubject line: Preaching Professor  \n \nAdditionally, please have three letters of recommendation submitted directly from the recommender to: \ndsweeney@samford.edu with the subject line denoting: “LAST NAME, FIRST NAME – Preaching \nProfessor”    \n \n   \nThe “Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty- employment.pdf \n \nThe “Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant- Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n \nQuestions may be addressed to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Doug Sweeney \n(dsweeney@samford.edu). \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, \nTitle IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans \nwith Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, \ngender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other \nprotected status under federal, state or local law applicable to the University, in its education policies, \nprograms, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of \nthe University. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations \nconcerning discrimination. \n \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/U-Lib-Metadata-and-Discovery-Librarian-for-Electronic-Resources-and-Serials-final.pdf": {
      "title": "(12-month, non-tenure track)",
      "description": " \nSamford University Library \n(12-month, non-tenure track)  \nSeptember 1, 2026 \n \nPosition: Metadata and Discovery Librarian for Electronic Resources and Serials \n \nStart Date: Immediately upon the completion of the candidate search process.  \n \nThe Samford University Library is seeking an energetic and innovative individual to serve as the Metadata \nand Discovery Librarian for Electronic Resources and Serials.  \n \nThe librarian in this position reports to the chair of the Cataloging Department, which is composed of three \nlibrarians and two staff members. The successful candidate will be responsible for managing the library’s \ndiscovery services and for establishing and maintaining comprehensive intellectual control of and access to \nelectronic resources and print serials at Samford University Library. Specific duties are listed below. \n \nResponsibilities \n• Manages and administers the library's web-scale discovery platform (EBSCO EDS). \n• Develops and maintains policies, procedures, and documentation related to discovery and electronic \nresources. \n• Identifies, troubleshoots, and resolves electronic resource and discovery issues. \n• Catalogs electronic resources and print serials to ensure accurate discovery and access. \n• Activates, updates, and maintains electronic resource collections and holdings. \n• Collects, analyzes, and interprets collection and usage statistics for annual university reporting and ACRL \nsurveys. \n• Coordinates with the Associate Dean (Chair of Collection Management) in all issues concerning the \nmanagement of electronic resources and serials. \n• Contributes to implementing the mission of Samford University Library and stay s abreast of emerging \ntrends and best practices related to electronic resources and serials. \n• Performs other duties as assigned. \n \nRequired qualifications \n• ALA-accredited master’s degree in library or information science. \n• One year of professional or high -level paraprofessional experience in technical services at an academic \nlibrary. \n• Experience with cataloging print serials and electronic resources. \n• Knowledge of web-scale discovery tools. \n• General understanding of electronic resources management tools such as OpenURL resolvers, proxy \nsystems, knowledge bases, and electronic resource management systems. \n• Knowledge of KBART. \n• Knowledge of MARC bibliographic and authority formats ; familiarity and ease with  Microsoft Excel ; \nfamiliarity with a bibliographic utility, preferably OCLC Connexion. \n• Familiarity with electronic resources tools, such as knowledgebases, link resolvers, and discovery \nservices. \n• Experience with both an integrated library system and an electronic resource management system. \n• Ability to identify and troubleshoot electronic resource access problems. \n• Ability to perform work with accuracy and perseverance; ability to work cooperatively and independently, \nwith initiative and good judgment. \n• Excellent interpersonal skills including oral and written communication and presentation skills. \n \n \n \n\nPreferred qualifications \n• Three years of professional or high -level paraprofessional experience in technical services at an academic \nlibrary. \n• Knowledge of RDA, Library of Congress Subject Headings, and Library of Congress Classification. \n• Familiarity with EBSCOadmin, EBSCONET, and EBSCO Experience Manager (EEM), OCLC \nConnexion, OCLC Local Holdings Maintenance, and COUNTER Release 5.1. \n• Knowledge of the Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP) and general guidelines for cataloging \nelectronic government document records. \n• Experience with system migrations. \n• Familiarity with the MarcEdit software. \n• Ability to implement and adapt to evolving technology. \n• Demonstrated ability to manage complex workflows and projects. \n \nRank and Salary \nTwelve‐month, non‐tenure track, faculty appointment; salary commensurate with experience; generous \nbenefits/vacation package; tuition remission available \n \nAbout Samford \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally \nrecognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in  1841, Samford enrolls 6,324 students from 44 \nstates, Puerto Rico and 16 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time faculty. Samford is ranked \n#3 in the nation for career preparation and #8 in the nation for student learning opportunities as l isted in \nThe Wall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry \nin a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith and offers \ncompetitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \n \nApplication Procedure \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until November 1 , 2026 . Candidates \nshould submit all application materials, including a letter of interest, current resume, Application for Faculty \nPosition, Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement, and  professional references . The successful \ncandidate must pass a background check, and applicants must be authorized to work in the United \nStates, as Samford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position.  \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \n \n“Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-\nStatement.docx \n \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to:     For questions, please email or call Jaroslaw Szurek at: \nFACAPP@samford.edu                                    jszurek@samford.edu; tel. (205) 726-4136 \nSubject: Metadata Librarian Faculty \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of \n1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the \nAmericans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on \nthe basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, \nreligion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law applicable to th e University, in its \neducation policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and \npractices, and all other areas of the University. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from \ncertain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/EDU-Teacher-Ed-Instr-Asst-Prof-Ad-TECH32-original-ns-an-final.pdf": {
      "title": "Instructor (NTT) or Assistant Professor (TT), Teacher Education",
      "description": " \nInstructor (NTT) or Assistant Professor (TT), Teacher Education \nDepartment of Teacher Education  \n(9-month appointment) \nOctober 20, 2026 \n \nSamford University’s Orlean Beeson School of Education invites individuals interested in serving in a Christian \nUniversity environment to apply for a full-time, 9-month position at the rank of Instructor, non-tenure track, or \nAssistant Professor, tenure track, in the Department of Teacher Education. This full-time, 9-month appointment \ncarries a twenty-four (24) credit-hour teaching load. The position will begin in August 2027. \nQualified candidates for the position of Instructor, non-tenure track, will hold at least an Educational Specialist \n(Ed.S.) degree in elementary education or a closely related field from an institutionally accredited college or \nuniversity (or equivalent). To be considered for the position of Assistant Professor, tenure track, candidates must \nhave a terminal degree in elementary education or a closely related field from an institutionally accredited college \nor university (or equivalent) and must demonstrate potential to develop and maintain an active scholarship stream. \nAll candidates must have at least five years’ teaching experience in an elementary school. In addition, candidates \nmust have one of the following: math coaching experience and/or certification; 18-hours of graduate level \ncoursework in mathematics; or documented math teaching experience at the elementary (K-6) level. Knowledge of \nAlabama’s Numeracy Act requirements and teaching experience in higher education are desirable but not required. \nExpectations for non-tenure-track faculty include teaching a 24-hour load per academic year and serving the \nuniversity, P-12 schools, and professional groups. Pursuing scholarship through professional publications, \npresentations, and grant-writing is optional for non-tenure track faculty.  \nThe Department of Teacher Education in the Orlean Beeson School of Education (samford.edu/education) is \nhome to Samford’s nationally accredited and award-winning educator preparation programs. \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally recognized \ngraduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls more than 6,500 students from 46 states, \nPuerto Rico and 12 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the \nnation for career preparation and #8 in the nation for student learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street \nJournal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. \nThe University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a \ngenerous benefits package. \n \nReview of applications will begin December 2, 2026, and applications will continue to be accepted until \nJanuary 16, 2027. Please submit (via email below in .pdf format), an application letter, curriculum vitae with \nnames and contact information of three references, description of teaching experience and interests, description of \nresearch plans if applying for tenure track position, along with an Application for Faculty Position and the Faculty \nApplicant Christian Mission Statement (links below). Salary is dependent upon qualifications and experience. The \nsuccessful candidate must pass a background check. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States, as \nSamford will not sponsor any employment visas for this position. \n \n\nApplication for Faculty Position can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf \nFaculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-Statement.docx \n \n \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to: \nFACAPP@samford.edu \nSubject: Teacher Education Faculty Position \n \nQuestions may be addressed to:  \nDr. Karen Birkenfeld, Search Committee Chair \nkjbirken@samford.edu \n \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title \nIX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans with \nDisabilities Act and ADA Amendments, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of sex, gender, \nrace, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any other protected \nstatus under federal, state or local law applicable to the University, in its education policies, programs, and \nactivities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As \na faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/SHP-Position-Description.pdf": {
      "title": "Dean of the School of Health Professions",
      "description": " \n \nDean of the School of Health Professions \nOctober 20, 2026 \n \nThe Opportunity \nSamford University seeks an experienced, mission-driven leader to serve as its next Dean \nof the School of Health Professions (SHP).  This school prepares health professionals \nthrough rigorous academic preparation, meaningful clinical experiences, and Christ-\ncentered professional formation. With nationally respected programs and strong \nconnections to healthcare providers throughout the region, the SHP contributes \nsignificantly to Samford's reputation for excellence in healthcare education. \nThe next Dean will join a university experiencing significant momentum in enrollment, \nphilanthropy, and academic distinction. The Dean will have the opportunity to strengthen \nexisting programs, support faculty excellence, expand strategic partnerships, advance \ninnovation in health professions education, and prepare graduates to lead with \ncompetence, compassion, and Christian conviction in an evolving healthcare environment.  \nThis is a distinctive opportunity to lead one of Samford University's flagship schools within \nthe College of Health Sciences and to shape the future of health professions education at \none of the nation's leading Christ-centered universities. \nThe School of Health Professions includes the following programs: \nUndergraduate: \nCommunication Sciences and Disorders \nExercise Science \nHealth Sciences \nSport Administration \nSports Medicine \n  \nGraduate: \nDoctor of Physical Therapy \nMS in Physician Assistant Studies \nMS in Speech-Language Pathology \nMS in Performance and Applied Sport Science \n \nThe Role \n\nThe Dean of the School of Health Professions serves as the chief academic and \nadministrative officer of the School of Health Professions and reports directly to the \nProvost and Vice President for Academic Affairs. The Dean provides vision and leadership \nfor all academic programs within the school, faculty recruitment and development, student \nsuccess, accreditation, budget oversight, and external engagement. The Dean will guide a \nschool distinguished by excellence in health professions education, interprofessional \ncollaboration, and fulfilling Samford’s Christ-centered mission through the integration of \nChristian faith and learning.  \nAs a member of the Deans Council, the Dean collaborates closely with colleagues across \nthe university and health sciences disciplines. The Dean will be a visible presence within \nthe Samford community, a trusted advocate for faculty, staff, and students, and a strategic \nleader who advances the School of Health Profession's reputation, impact, and growth. \nAbove all, the Dean will embody Samford University's Christ-centered mission while  \nfostering a culture of excellence in teaching, scholarship, clinical practice, and service.  \nPreferred Qualifications & Characteristics \nThe ideal candidate will: \n• Exemplify Christian faith and integrity, demonstrating the ability to advance \nSamford's Christ-centered mission through the work of the SHP. \n• Demonstrate a relational, servant-leadership style that fosters trust, collaboration, \nand a culture of excellence among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and clinical \npartners. \n• Hold an earned terminal degree in a discipline represented within the School of \nHealth Professions, with credentials meriting appointment at the rank of professor.  \n• Bring significant academic leadership experience, preferably as a department chair, \nassociate dean, dean, or equivalent administrator in higher education. \n• Possess a distinguished record of teaching, scholarship, clinical practice and/or \nprofessional service. \n• Demonstrate skills in faculty recruitment, mentoring, retention, promotion, and \nprofessional development. \n• Have experience with accreditation processes and continuous quality improvement \nin health professions education.  \n• Foster innovation in curriculum development, experiential learning, and \ninterprofessional education. \n• Demonstrate strong external engagement skills, including partnership development \nwith healthcare organizations, clinical agencies, community stakeholders, and \nprofessional associations. \n• Possess a record of successful fundraising, alumni engagement, and advancement \nactivities. \n• Exhibit strong financial and operational acumen, with experience overseeing \nbudgets, facilities, strategic planning, and resource allocation. \n• Communicate effectively and inspire confidence through clarity of vision, \nauthenticity, and collaboration. \nDuties & Responsibilities \nIn addition to special assignments by the Provost, the Dean will: \n• Provide strategic leadership for the School of Health Professions, articulating and \nimplementing a clear vision that advances academic excellence, enrollment \ngrowth, and mission fulfillment. \n• Recruit, support, evaluate, and develop faculty and staff while fostering excellence \nin teaching, scholarship, clinical education, and service. \n• Oversee accreditation processes, assessment efforts, and compliance with \nprofessional and institutional standards. \n• Advance interprofessional education and collaboration throughout the College of \nHealth Sciences. \n• Strengthen student recruitment, retention, professional formation, and career \nsuccess. \n• Promote innovation in academic programming, clinical education, and community \nengagement. \n• Manage budgets, resources, personnel, and facilities with transparency, \nstewardship, and accountability. \n• Build and sustain partnerships with healthcare systems, rehabilitation providers, \nclinical agencies, schools, community organizations, and professional \nassociations. \n• Advance alumni engagement, donor relations, and philanthropic support for the \nSHP's programs and strategic priorities. \n• Represent the School with external constituencies while enhancing its regional and \nnational reputation. \n• Serve as an active member of the Deans Council, collaborating with university \nleadership to achieve institutional goals. \n• Model Christian leadership, integrity, service, and the integration of faith and \nlearning consistent with Samford University's mission and values.  \nAbout Samford \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford ( samford.edu) is a \nleading Christian university offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts \nwith an array of nationally recognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, \nSamford enrolls more than 6,500 students from 4 6 states, Puerto Rico and 12 countries in \nits 10 academic schools with 450 full -time faculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for \ncareer preparation and #7 in the nation for student  learning opportunities as listed in The \nWall Street Journal (2026). The University is dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic \ninquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and prefers employee applicants of the \nChristian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits package. \n \nApplication Process \nApplication materials should include a cover letter detailing teaching and professional \ninterests and areas of expertise, a curriculum vitae, a completed faculty application (link \nbelow), a faculty applicant Christian Mission Statement (link below), and a list of three \nreferences with contact information. Salary is competitive. The successful candidate must \npass a background check and must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford \ndoes not provide for visa sponsorship. Samford University is committed to fostering a vibrant \nlearning environment among faculty, students, and staff, and strongly enco urages \napplications from individuals who will contribute to the academic richness of the Samford \ncommunity.  Applications will be accepted until December 1, 2026. \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-\nemployment.pdf \n \nand the “Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-\nChristian-Mission-Statement.docx \n  \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to: \nFACAPP@samford.edu \nSubject: Health Professions Dean \nFor questions about the position please contact: \nDr. Dawn McCormack, Search Committee Co-Chair \nmmccorm5@samford.edu \n \nDr. Suresh Mathews, Search Committee Co-Chair \nsmathew1@samford.edu \n \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act \nof 1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, \nand the Americans with Disabilities Act and ADA Amendme nts, the University does not unlawfully \ndiscriminate on the basis of sex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic \ninformation, veteran status, religion, or any other protected status under federal, state or local law \napplicable to th e University, in its education policies, programs, and activities, in its admissions \npolicies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas of the University. As a faith-based \ninstitution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regulations concerning discrimination. \n "
    },
    "https://www.samford.edu/employee/faculty-employment-opportunities/SPH-SW-Asst-Assoc-2026-final.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR  \nDEPARTMENT OF SOCIAL WORK \n(9-month, Tenure-Track) \nOctober 19, 2026 \nSamford University’s School of Public Health invites applicants interested in serving in a Christian university \nenvironment to apply for the position of Assistant/Associate Professor in the Department of Social Work. \nThis full-time, tenure track position is a 9-month appointment in the graduate program with a tentative \nJanuary or August 2027 start date. This position will have faculty and leadership responsibilities including \nteaching online and face-to-face, student mentorship, service, course development, and scholarship. The \ndepartment has an advanced clinical concentration which focuses on micro-level practice, counseling and \npsychotherapy with individuals and families. The Council on Social Work Education accredited the Master of \nSocial Work program in 2017 as a face-to-face program and approved the addition of a fully online MSW \nprogram in 2019. \n \nQualified candidates will hold an MSW from an accredited CSWE program, a doctoral degree from an \ninstitutionally accredited university and a minimum of three years post-masters experience in a clinical \nsetting or private practice. Current Alabama State License (LICSW, PIP preferred) or license eligible. Ideal \ncandidates will be able to teach courses across the curriculum both face to face or online, design and \ndevelop innovative online instruction, provide mentorship for students, participate in interprofessional \nsimulation, and engage in service at the local and state level. A commitment to academic excellence and \nthe ability to work effectively with faculty, students and colleagues is expected. To qualify for the rank of \nassociate professor, a successful candidate will have at least 6 years of previous academic experience in \nhigher education with a demonstrated record of scholarship, teaching, and service.  \n \nThe School of Public Health is housed in the College of Health Sciences to include the Moffett and Sanders \nSchool of Nursing, McWhorter School of Pharmacy, and School of Health Professions. The College offers \nmultiple degree programs and majors within the health sciences with a focus on inter-professional \neducation that reflects today’s team approach to health care delivery and professional practice. The \nDepartment of Social Work in the School of Public Health offers a MSW along with several dual-degree and \nfast-track programs. For more information, see the Department’s web page: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/publichealth/social-work \n \nLocated in the Homewood suburb of Birmingham, Alabama, Samford (samford.edu) is a leading Christian \nuniversity offering undergraduate programs grounded in the liberal arts with an array of nationally \nrecognized graduate and professional schools. Founded in 1841, Samford enrolls more than 6,500 \nstudents from 46 states, Puerto Rico and 12 countries in its 10 academic schools with 450 full-time \nfaculty. Samford is ranked #3 in the nation for career preparation. #5 for most recommended colleges, and \n#7 in the nation for student learning opportunities as listed in The Wall Street Journal (2026). The University \nis dedicated to the promotion of rigorous academic inquiry in a Christian setting. The University seeks and \nprefers employee applicants of the Christian faith and offers competitive salaries with a generous benefits \npackage. \n \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue through November 15, 2026. Please submit \na cover letter, curriculum vita with names and contact information of three professional references, \nevidence of teaching effectiveness, description of research interests and activities, along with an \nApplication for Faculty Position, and Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement (links shown below). \nAcademic rank and salary are dependent upon qualifications and experience. The successful candidate \nmust pass a background check. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States, as Samford will \nnot sponsor any employment visas for this position.  \n\n \n \n“Application for Faculty Position” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/Human_Resources/application-for-faculty-employment.pdf  \n \nand the “Faculty Applicant Christian Mission Statement” can be found at: \nhttps://www.samford.edu/departments/files/academic-affairs/Faculty-Applicant-Christian-Mission-Statement.docx \n \n \nPlease submit all materials in PDF format to: \nFACAPP@samford.edu  \nATTN: Asst/Assoc Prof Social Work \n \nFor questions about the position contact:  \nDr. Rachel Hagues, Search Committee Chair \nEmail: rhagues@samford.edu \n     \nIn accordance with applicable federal and state laws, such as Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, \nTitle IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, and the Americans \nwith Disabilities Act and ADA Amendme nts, the University does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of \nsex, gender, race, color, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, religion, or any \nother protected status under federal, state or local law applicable to th e University, in its education policies, \nprograms, and activities, in its admissions policies, in employment policies and practices, and all other areas \nof the University. As a faith-based institution, the University is exempted from certain laws and regul ations \nconcerning discrimination. \n "
    }
  },
